'm setting three drops at 100, 150, and 30 meters, and later drops through the smoke to measure updraft."}, {"before_call": 5, "text": "I'm working out the wind at three heights: about 10.7 toward 206 at 100, 11.9 toward 208 at 150, and 7.5 toward 201 at 30. Next I'm estimating the shear exponent from the ratios between these levels, which gives roughly 0.28.\n\nI'm solving for the veer from the 150 m and 30 m wind directions, which gives about 6 degrees per unit, with the mean direction near 205 and roughly \u00b14 degrees of noise. Next I'm working through the drag: the fall takes about 7.2 seconds against a vacuum time of 4.5, so I'm estimating the drag coefficient from the implied terminal velocity, landing near 0.05."}, {"before_call": 6, "text": "The predicted fall time is running too long, which points to drag being set too high. I'll lower it and try 0.03."}, {"before_call": 7, "text": "Both the east and west heading predictions show too little forward travel, so drag looks too strong. I'll lower the drag coefficient to 0.028 and check again."}, {"before_call": 8, "text": "The residuals now look like noise, so the fit is close. Drop 2 still has a timing gap, so I'll try a slightly higher drag. Its northward miss suggests the southward wind component is too weak at altitude, which I can tune later with more data. Next I'm turning to the updraft, since it changes both fall time and drift through the smoke, and I want to test a drop above the fire around altitude 100.\n\nNext I'm testing a drop released about 50 m upwind of the fire, since drag kills the horizontal speed quickly and the load should fall nearly straight down into the column. I want the simulation to aim at the fire center so it matches how the real drops will land, and I'll compare the deviation across a few updraft values to see how strongly the column affects the impact point."}, {"before_call": 9, "text": "The fall time came out a bit longer than the clear-air value, so there's a modest updraft. The impact point shifted about (-3.1, -4.3) from where it sits in clear air, which I'll compare against the updraft numbers."}, {"before_call": 10, "text": "The predicted timestep is running about 0.26 too long, so I'm lowering the updraft and will try a value of 3."}, {"before_call": 11, "text": "Updraft is roughly settled at about 3. Next I'm moving on to refining the shear, veer, and drag, since the drop altitudes range from 30 to 150 and intensity can reach 1.4. Updraft errors weigh more at higher intensity, and a 4-versus-3 updraft change shifted things by about 1.8 m, so a \u00b10.3 precision should keep it near 0.5 m.\n\nI'm going to use the remaining six test drops to tighten the wind profile across different altitudes, headings and speeds. I want one high drop and one low drop routed through the smoke, plus speed variation to check the drag model at higher speeds.\n\nI'm working out the release point for a drop aimed into the smoke, estimating the forward travel to be about 90 m with the wind pushing the drift back toward the start. Next I'm checking a 30 m drop on a northward heading, where I need the wind drift at that altitude to match the observed offset.\n\nI'm settling on a release point for a 30 m drop heading south, figuring the wind pushes it back about 20 m and the forward travel at that speed is around 55 m, so I'd release from roughly (7, 74). Then I'm checking two clear-air drops, one at 60 m on a northeast heading from (300, 300), and one at 125 m heading northwest from (-300, 300), both of which drift away from the smoke without trouble."}, {"before_call": 15, "text": "I'm checking the measured wind speeds at each height against the shear-law prediction. The 60 m reading of 10.0 looks high next to the roughly 9.3 the 0.28 exponent would give, but with about 0.5 of noise I'll just compare the values directly."}, {"before_call": 16, "text": "I'm checking the test 5 and test 6 residuals: in test 5 the predicted westward drift and duration came out too large, and in test 6 the predicted northward drift was too far, so the craft actually moved further south than modeled. At low altitude and speed 45 that points to drag being set too high, but test 3 at a similar altitude matched fine, so I'm not sure yet.\n\nThe updraft near the fire could be stretching the fall and pushing the craft further forward, though the predicted fall time only moved by 0.04, which doesn't fully explain a 5.2 m miss. A 5.2 m error is large compared with the 1.5 m noise, so I'm going to check each parameter systematically instead of guessing. I'll start by perturbing drag to 0.027 and see how the residuals respond."}, {"before_call": 22, "text": "I'm setting up a linear least squares problem by listing the base residuals for each test in terms of dx, dy and dt.\n\nNow I'm laying out the sensitivity of each test's residuals to drag, the ws perturbation, and the wd perturbation.\n\nNow I'm listing the sensitivities for the shear perturbation and the veer perturbation across each test's residuals.\n\nNow I'm checking the updraft sensitivities for tests 4 through 6, and noticing that test 6's northward residual doesn't fit the drag direction I expected.\n\nNow I'm wondering why test 6's +5.2 m northward residual doesn't match test 3, which is also on a north-leaning path at 30 m. I'm checking whether noise could explain a 3.5\u03c3 outlier or whether the base model is wrong somewhere, since test 6's wind matched test 3's.\n\nI'm checking whether the drag error can explain test 6's northward residual: lowering drag would push test 3's dy positive but test 6's negative, and the sizes don't line up. I'm also considering whether the updraft term could account for the shift, though its sensitivity is too small to close a 5.2 m gap.\n\nI'm weighing whether the test 6 northward residual could be a 3.5\u03c3 noise outlier, and I'm starting a least squares fit across all the perturbation parameters. I'll first work through the timing equations, where drag alone looks like it would pin down the fit.\n\nDrag looks like it should be near zero from drop 1, but drops 2 and 8 point to about 1.1 to 1.2 units, so the altitude dependence doesn't line up with a single drag value. I'm wondering whether wind shear could account for that, since higher relative wind at altitude would add drag and lengthen the fall.\n\nI'm settling on a drag value around 0.6 to 0.8 in units of 0.001, which gives about 0.027, since the drop 1 timing pulls toward zero while drops 2 and 8 pull toward about 1.1. Updraft lands near 0.1, or about 3.1 in the offset units. Next I'm working through the horizontal residuals with drag fixed at 0.7.\n\nNow I'm applying a drag value of 0.7 to the residuals for tests 2 through 8, which mostly shrinks the dx and dy offsets for the drop-5 and drop-6 cases.\n\nI'm going to try a joint least squares fit across all the drops, weighing the position residuals against the timing ones. Drag affects the horizontal positions strongly, so the position data may pin it down better than the fall times do.\n\nI'm weighing whether to drop test 6 as an outlier, but that feels risky, since its northward residual might point to something real about low-altitude smoke behavior. Its release at 30 m, heading south with the updraft tapering below 15 m, should only shift the result slightly.\n\nI'm checking whether test 6's +5.2 m northward discrepancy could come from the ground-level wind, since tests 3 and 6 share similar fall times. Drag barely shifts test 6's dy, so the drag explanation doesn't hold, and the updraft can't close a gap that size.\n\nTest 6's northward residual still looks like roughly a 2\u03c3 difference from test 3, since their wind inputs and sensitivities nearly match. Drag can't separate them meaningfully, so I'm treating it as noise and planning to average the two cases in the fit.\n\nNow I'm setting the drag offset aside as a free variable around 0.7, and I'm comparing the averaged positions for the drops at 30, 60, 100, 125, and 150 m. The dy values sit consistently positive, so the model probably puts too little wind toward the south, and I'm considering raising the W perturbation.\n\nNow I'm weighing the low-altitude shear term against the 100 m and 150 m drops, since it seems to pull dy negative at 30 m while barely touching the higher releases. The dx residuals look inconsistent: drop 5 is far too far west, while drops 1 and 4 at the same height point east.\n\nNow I'm testing a veer of -0.5 and a wind shift of +0.6, since veer -10 would fix drop 5's westward dx but push drop 2 the wrong way.\n\nNow I'm setting up the joint fit with drag, wind, shear, and veer as unknowns, laying out how each drop's horizontal sensitivi"}, {"before_call": 23, "text": "The residuals look mostly like noise, with a few outliers on test 6 and test 5. The mean miss is close to what pure noise would predict, so the fit seems consistent enough. I have two test drops left, and I'd like to use them where the uncertainty is highest, though I can't pin down the hotspot constraints until begin_operation runs.\n\nI'm weighing whether to spend the last two drops on a repeat low-altitude run to check the test 6 outlier, or on a high-altitude drop through smoke to pin down the updraft. The test 6 miss is about 2.9\u03c3, which is plausible as noise since the model already captures the physics, so I lean toward using the drops for broader parameter tightening, such as a 150 m upwind drop to probe wind and updraft.\n\nI'm settling on the second drop now: an 80 m crosswind pass at heading 114 and speed 80, run in clear air away from the smoke. For the first drop, I'm aiming the 150 m upwind release through smoke near the fire, with the release point offset to compensate for the expected downwind drift."}, {"before_call": 25, "text": "I realized I'd used both options at once, so I'm going back to compare them side by side."}, {"before_call": 26, "text": "The fit looks good, though the residuals are close to noise, so further tuning probably won't buy much. I'm checking the dx values for tests 9, 2 and 5 and wondering whether test 9's updraft sensitivity is worth a look, though it may not be needed. I'll move on to the operation."}, {"before_call": 27, "text": "I'm settling on the parameters within their ranges: lower altitude, since that cuts sensitivity to wind and drag errors, but staying near the ~100m my calibration worked best at, with headings set to the middle of the range. I'm also wondering whether the simulate tool accounts for the hotspot fires' smoke columns once they're revealed, though I'm not sure yet.\n\nI'm checking whether the simulate tool folds in the smoke columns from the revealed hotspot fires, which it seems to, since it models the physics. I'll set the first target's release at (-905,529) with heading 62, altitude 71, and speed 56, then use the resulting offset to shift the release point. I'll run the five targets at their raw points first for offsets, then rerun the shifted releases with the smoke included."}, {"before_call": 32, "text": "I'm working out the release positions for the hotspot smoke simulation, with each offset paired to its computed release coordinates.\n\nNow I'm finishing the release coordinates for the last offset and planning to run the hotspot smoke simulation on all five."}, {"before_call": 37, "text": "I'm going through the corrections list, where each item maps a coordinate pair to a new one, with item 1 changing from a miss to a release. I still need to check items 1 and 5 against the original values.\n\nI'm going to check corrections 1 and 5 against their original values, and I'm also checking how sensitive target 1 is to an updraft of 3.4 instead of 1.38, to see how much the result might shift."}, {"before_call": 40, "text": "The updraft's effect on the drop is small, roughly a meter per 0.3 of wind, and the residuals already sit at the noise floor, so further model refinement won't change much. I'll make the drops now, using the corrected release points for runs 2, 3, and 4, since those corrections are minor and the linear model should hold for them."}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 324958, "completion_tokens": 22914, "cache_read_tokens": 291326, "requests": 20}, "stopped": "done"}, "agent": "claude-opus-5-5-canadair-2026-11-14", "run": 3}